---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE_TITLE, AUTHOR } from '../consts';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description={`What ${SITE_TITLE} is, who writes it, and the principles behind how it is built.`}
>
  <div class="page about">
    <h1>About</h1>

    <div class="about__body">
      <p class="lede">
        {SITE_TITLE} is a personal mathematics publication written by {AUTHOR}. It exists because
        the explanations that helped me most were the ones that refused to choose between intuition
        and rigour, and there are not enough of them.
      </p>

      <h2>What you will find here</h2>
      <p>
        Long-form essays on calculus, linear algebra, probability, statistics, discrete mathematics
        and optimization, with a bias towards the mathematics that machine learning actually uses.
        An article typically moves from a concrete problem, to intuition, to a formal definition, to
        a derivation, and only then to the interactive figures.
      </p>

      <h2>How the figures are meant to be used</h2>
      <p>
        Every interactive figure is a claim you can test. Each one names the quantity you control,
        shows the numbers it produces, and states in its caption what you should notice. If a figure
        cannot load — or if you have JavaScript switched off — the surrounding text still contains
        the full argument. Nothing mathematical is available only through animation.
      </p>

      <h2>How the site is built</h2>
      <p>
        Static HTML generated by Astro, with TypeScript for the visualizations and KaTeX for the
        mathematics. There is no framework runtime, no tracking, no cookies, no fonts fetched from
        someone else's server, and no code that runs before you scroll to a figure that needs it.
        Animation stops when a figure leaves the screen or the tab is hidden, and
        <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> replaces animation with step-by-step controls.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Mathematical writing has errors in it; mine will too. Articles carry an “updated” date when
        they change substantively. If you find a mistake, please do report it — a correction is more
        useful to me than a compliment.
      </p>

      <p class="about__links">
        <a class="link-arrow" href="/blog/">Read the articles</a>
        <a class="link-arrow" href="/rss.xml">Subscribe by RSS</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about {
    padding-block: var(--s-7) var(--s-8);
    max-width: calc(var(--w-prose) + var(--gutter) * 2);
  }
  h1 {
    font-family: var(--f-serif);
    font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 1.4rem + 2vw, 2.6rem);
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .about__body {
    font-family: var(--f-serif);
    font-size: var(--fs-prose);
    line-height: var(--lh-prose);
    color: var(--c-ink);
    margin-block-start: var(--s-5);
  }
  .about__body > * + * {
    margin-block-start: var(--s-4);
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: clamp(1.075rem, 1rem + 0.5vw, 1.2rem);
    color: var(--c-ink-soft);
  }
  h2 {
    font-family: var(--f-sans);
    font-size: var(--fs-md);
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-block-start: var(--s-6) !important;
    color: var(--c-ink);
  }
  code {
    font-family: var(--f-mono);
    font-size: 0.86em;
    background: var(--c-bg-sunk);
    border: 1px solid var(--c-rule);
    border-radius: 3px;
    padding: 0.1em 0.32em;
  }
  .about__links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--s-5);
    margin-block-start: var(--s-7) !important;
    border-top: var(--border);
    padding-block-start: var(--s-5);
  }
</style>
